//! Error: SENTINEL FILE -- every case here is an UNBUILT MECHANISM, not a
//!        rejection we want to keep. Each one pins a `nobox` container gap that
//!        is live today so it cannot rot in a doc; when the mechanism lands the
//!        case stops rejecting, THIS FILE GOES RED, and the fix's author must
//!        DELETE the case and replace it with a positive test (a `.rock` @test
//!        asserting the operation works). Do not "fix" a red case by relaxing
//!        the expectation.
//!
//!        Measured against the real `nobox value Vec2`. What WORKS today, for
//!        contrast, so nobody re-derives it: `[Vec2]` is genuinely column-stored
//!        (`__rock_is_column_vec` = true) and supports index / get / push / pop /
//!        set / insert_at / remove_at / swap_remove / clear / clone / sort / for /
//!        enumerate / find / any / all / min_by / count, plus the whole lazy
//!        combinator pipeline (map / filter / collect / reduce / zip / skip /
//!        take / chains) -- positive coverage in
//!        `tests/nobox/column_combinators.rock`. `[Vec2?]` stores a PRESENCE
//!        column beside the leaf columns and supports that same Vec surface
//!        EXCEPT the five iterator terminals (`tests/nobox/nullable_column.rock`).
//!        The whole lazy-combinator surface over a NULLABLE column works too --
//!        fifteen shapes asserted as values in
//!        `tests/nobox/nullable_column_combinators.rock`, including `zip`'s
//!        tuple element. A TUPLE of nobox at a fn param works
//!        (`tests/nobox/tuple.rock`), and so does a chained `?.` through
//!        two presence levels (`tests/nobox/safe_nav.rock`). A NULLABLE tuple
//!        carrying one -- `(Vec2, int)?`, from a declared return or from a `?.`
//!        -- binds its wire and supports `== null` / slot projection / `if let`
//!        (binding AND destructuring) / `??` / rebinding / forwarding, in
//!        `tests/nobox/nullable_tuple.rock`; it crosses a fn PARAM in every
//!        definition form and calling convention, in
//!        the same file. `a.enumerate()` on
//!        a column vec works now that the eager `Vec<T>.enumerate` is deleted --
//!        it coerces to the lazy one (`tests/nobox/column_combinators.rock`).
//!        `Arena<Vec2>` supports insert / get / contains /
//!        remove / len / clear / pairs / values / keys / enumerate. A REFERENCE
//!        struct field holding a nobox whose own field is nobox splices its
//!        nested leaves both ways (`tests/nobox/nested_ctor.rock`), which is
//!        what closed `?.` yielding one -- safe-nav was never the variable.
//!
//!        `inspect` and `to_lua_array` over a column `[Vec2]` also reject and
//!        are deliberately NOT in this file: those are F1 erasure, a justified
//!        box, and are meant to stay rejections.
//! Tags: nobox, collections, columns, arena

//=== TEST CASE: whole_tuple_escape_of_a_nobox_Generic ===
// NOT an unbuilt mechanism -- the guarantee firing, pinned so the boundary
// cannot move unnoticed. A nullable tuple carrying a nobox element is bound and
// used slot-wise (seventeen shapes in `tests/nobox/nullable_tuple.rock`), but
// interpolating the WHOLE tuple asks for one value with a runtime type, which is
// a box by definition -- the same justified-box class as `inspect` over a column
// vec. Project the slots instead (`t.0.x`), or `materialize` deliberately.
nobox value SNV { x: int, y: int }
fn maybe(b: bool) -> (SNV, int)? {
    if b { return (SNV { x: 3, y: 6 }, 1) }
    return null
}
fn main() {
    let t = maybe(true)
    print("{t}")   // ERROR: the whole tuple escapes, so its nobox element boxes
}

//=== TEST CASE: inline_tuple_interpolation_of_a_nobox_Generic ===
// The same boundary reached WITHOUT a binding. It rejects through a different
// site (`stringify_non_null`, not the packed repr) and is pinned separately
// because it once compiled while the bound form above rejected: the box had
// already widened, so `inspect` walked a 3-slot table and would have rendered a
// 3-tuple. That is a silent wrong answer, which the widened box cannot leave to
// the residue check -- there is no `<name>_ct` left for it to find.
nobox value INV { x: float, y: float }
fn inv(k: float) -> INV { return INV { x: k, y: 2.0 } }
fn main() {
    print("{(inv(1.0), 1.0)}")   // ERROR: the widened box would render as a wider tuple
}

//=== TEST CASE: direct_inspect_of_a_tuple_carrying_a_nobox_Generic ===
// The call-boundary spelling of the case above: `inspect` reached by name rather
// than through interpolation. Pinned as its own case because the two are checked
// at different sites and only one of them used to exist.
nobox value DNV { x: float, y: float }
fn dnv(k: float) -> DNV { return DNV { x: k, y: 2.0 } }
fn main() {
    print(inspect((dnv(1.0), 1.0)))   // ERROR: same widened box, reached by call
}
